Title: Pause the sitter while Zotero is syncing files
Created: 2026-09-15
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-15T19:46Z MinhHaDuong created — proposed by the author while watching a live sync on padme: "Pauser quand ça sync ?"
2026-09-15T19:46Z claude note Observed tonight on padme, v0.4.16, Zotero 10.0.2, three libraries. The panel read "Stored or linked file unavailable 13 699" against "Indexed and up to date 2", and both the author and this session first read it as a defect. It was not: `storage/` held 688 attachment folders at 19:39 and 1 285 at 19:43, growing ~1 000 files/minute — the census had classified a library whose files were ~9 % present. The reconciliation line said "last full pass, 3 min 43 s ago" and was correct. An age in minutes does not warn that the data behind it is 95 % obsolete.
2026-09-15T19:53Z claude note Live evidence, and it moves the ticket's centre of gravity: the cost is not the display. The author's diagnostics dump, taken during the sync, shows `pump()` inside the drain loop for 8 minutes with no `drain-end` — `busy` true, `active` null, `phase: draining`, candidate queue frozen at 44. The drain calls `host.unattached()` once per dirty id (`scheduler.js:215-218`, a library-wide query its own comment at 193-195 already calls "long on its own account") while `invalidate()` refills `dirty` from the sync's item notifications. Candidate reading: a race the drain loses, not a wedge. Record and analysis in `verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md`; the discriminating measurement (three reads of `state.draining`) is named there and was NOT taken.
2026-09-15T19:59Z claude note Correction to the 19:53Z note, and the reason 0796 exists. "A race the drain loses, not a wedge" was wrong. Three live reads of `state.draining` — 6495, 6543, 6573, against 761 at `drain-start` twenty-five minutes earlier — show the backlog growing 8.5x, and the structure explains why the sitter cannot recover: the drain at `scheduler.js:201` is unbounded, so `refreshQueue()` (99, called only at 176) never re-runs and admission (228+) is never reached. `pending: 44` was a photograph from 19:39, not a live count. This ticket still stands on its own terms — pausing during a sync is right, and it removes the common trigger — but the loop is unsafe with or without it, and that is 0796.

2026-09-15T20:43Z claude note Action 1 now states what this session learned trying to take the measurement and failing: neither arm is available on demand. The reading needs a live Zotero window with the Run JavaScript pane, so no headless session and no RDP client can take it unless the instance was launched with --start-debugger-server, and relaunching for the flag destroys the state under observation. The active arm additionally needs a real bulk sync, which is hours of transfer and starves the sitter until 0796 is fixed. The evening's instance shut down at 20:31:54Z with the plugin uninstalled before either arm was taken. Ticket 0797 action 4 points here, so the constraint belongs in the ticket rather than in a session's hope.
--- body ---
## Context

During a bulk file sync, every attachment Zotero has not yet downloaded reads
as `missing-source`: `bootstrap.js:3207-3216` sets that status when
`getFilePathAsync()` returns nothing or `IOUtils.stat()` throws, which is the
correct answer about a file that is genuinely not on disk yet. The census is
therefore not wrong — it is a true account of a moment that the sync makes
obsolete within minutes.

Two costs, and the second is the one that matters:

1. The work is wasted. Classifying 13 700 attachments that are about to change
   state, then re-classifying them, is exactly the footprint SPEC.md's "quiet
   supervisor" paragraph exists to bound (ticket 0701).
2. **The display misleads.** A reader sees a full categorical breakdown, stated
   with the same confidence as in steady state, beside an age of three minutes.
   Nothing in the panel says the classification was taken against a library
   that was 9 % present. Tonight this misled the author and this session at
   once, and only measuring the filesystem settled it.

## What already exists, and must be reused

- `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` (`bootstrap.js:718`) already holds five refusal phases,
  and ticket **0745** already made a blocked phase back off to the 10-minute
  idle cadence instead of the 30-second active one. A sixth phase inherits
  both the backoff and the phase label. Nothing new is needed for the
  mechanism — only for the detection.
- Ticket **0759**'s ruling governs the display half: *"RULING: live. Accept the
  truth and disclose it, rather than hiding or debouncing it."* So a pause must
  not merely freeze the numbers. The phase label is what discloses the reason,
  and that is the half of this change the ruling makes mandatory rather than
  optional.

## The unknown, and why it is measured before anything is designed

The sitter has **zero** references to `Zotero.Sync`, `syncInProgress` or
`Sync.Runner` in `bootstrap.js` and `scheduler.js`. Whether Zotero 10 exposes a
readable sync state to a bootstrap extension is unestablished here.

This matters more than the usual missing-API question because the five existing
phases all read the **machine** (`/proc/meminfo`, disk, CPU) while this one
would read the **host application** — the contract class where this codebase
has already been burned once: `bootstrap.js:3190-3196` carries a guard written
against an assumed host contract that made every attachment read as a linked
file. A phase that cannot fire, or that fires always, is worse than no phase.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` (718),
  `nextSweepDelayMS`, `blocked()` (~3333-3360), the phase label table (~350)
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `record()`, `publish()`; sets `phase`
  from `host.blocked()`
- `tests/sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` — the mock host; see the warning below
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py`, `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs`

## Actions

1. **Measure the host contract first.** In the author's Zotero console, while a
   sync is running, enumerate what is readable rather than guessing a name:

   ```js
   (() => {
     const R = Zotero.Sync?.Runner, S = Zotero.Sync?.Storage;
     return JSON.stringify({
       runner: !!R,
       syncInProgress: R?.syncInProgress ?? null,
       runnerKeys: R ? Object.keys(R).filter(k => !k.startsWith('_')) : null,
       storageKeys: S ? Object.keys(S) : null,
       localKeys: S?.Local ? Object.keys(S.Local).filter(k => !k.startsWith('_')) : null,
     }, null, 2);
   })()
   ```

   Record the output in this ticket. Re-run it with sync idle: a key that reads
   the same in both states does not discriminate and must not be used.

   **Neither arm is available on demand, and the active one is the scarce half.**
   The reading needs a live Zotero window with the Run JavaScript pane — no
   headless session, no RDP client against an instance that was not launched with
   `--start-debugger-server`, can take it, and relaunching for the flag destroys
   whatever state was being observed. The idle arm only costs a session. The
   active arm additionally needs a real bulk sync in progress, which is not
   something to provoke: it is hours of transfer and, until 0796 is fixed, it
   starves the sitter for the duration. So this measurement waits for the next
   sync that happens anyway. Plan the ticket around catching one, not around
   scheduling one.
2. Decide the fallback when the API is absent or unreadable. The default this
   ticket proposes: behave exactly as today. A sitter that pauses because it
   cannot tell is a sitter that never runs.
3. Add the phase, its label, and its entry in `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`.
4. Ensure a sync ending wakes the sitter rather than leaving it parked for the
   full 10-minute idle interval.

## Test

Red step, in the scheduler suite: a mock host reporting a sync in progress must
leave `phase` at the new value and `nextSweepDelayMS` at the idle cadence.
Reverting the `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` entry must reproduce the active cadence —
the same red-first shape ticket 0745 used for the five existing phases.

**The mock must not invent the platform.** `bootstrap.js:3190-3196` records
what happens when the harness publishes a host API the real Zotero shapes
differently: every fixture read as a linked file and the defect was invisible
until a real host disagreed. Shape the mock from the step-1 output, not from
what the API ought to look like.

## Decision for the author

**On a fresh install the first run is a large sync, so this pauses the sitter
for precisely the session where a new user is watching it.** Indexing files
that are still arriving is wasted work — they are re-hashed once settled — but
the cost is that first value arrives later, and the panel says "waiting" during
the one session that decides whether the plugin looks alive.

Accepting that is the proposal. The alternative is to pause the **census** and
let admission keep indexing whatever is already on disk, which keeps the
counter moving at the price of a second, narrower rule. Not chosen here.

## Invariants

- The five existing phases and 0745's backoff keep their current behaviour.
- 0759 stands: totals stay live and disclosed, never debounced or hidden.
- No new library write, and no new periodic cost while idle.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Step 1's console output recorded in this ticket, with the sync-idle
      control, and a named key that discriminates between the two states
- [ ] The phase ships with a label, or the ticket closes with the measured
      reason it cannot
- [ ] Red-first test in the scheduler suite, reverting the entry reproduces the
      active cadence
- [ ] Sync completion wakes the sitter without waiting out the idle interval
- [ ] Three reads of `state.draining` on a live syncing instance, recorded in
      `verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md`: falling
      confirms the race reading, holding or rising splits a new ticket off
- [ ] `make check` passes
